(2,1)-(3,1):\\;0,\\\\\n  &(1,1)-(1,3):\\;\\infty,\\\\\n  &(1,3)-(2,2),\\;(2,2)-(3,1):\\;-1,\\\\\n  &(1,1)-(2,2):\\;1\\ (\\text{sunny}),\\text{ but the third point }(3,1)\n     \\text{ is not collinear with them.}\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n  Consequently a sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(T\\); to\n  cover the five points with only two sunny lines we would need a sunny\n  line containing three points, which is impossible because **no** triple\n  of points from \\(T\\) is collinear with an allowed slope (the only\n  possible slope \\(1\\) occurs for the pair \\((1,1),(2,2)\\) and does not\n  include any third point of \\(T\\)).  Hence a contradiction.\n\nThus a horizontal non\u2011sunny line cannot be part of a valid configuration.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  \\(L_{0}\\) vertical:  \\(L_{0}:x=c\\)\n\nThe only vertical lines meeting \\(S\\) are \\(x=1,2,3\\).\n\n* **(i) \\(c=1\\).**  \n  \\(L_{0}\\) contains \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\).  \n  Hence  \n\n  \\[\n  T=\\{(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n  \\]\n\n  The three pairs give slopes \\(\\infty,0,-1\\), all forbidden; each sunny\n  line can cover at most one point, but \\(|T|=3\\).  Contradiction.\n\n* **(ii) \\(c=3\\).**  \n  Symmetric to the case \\(c=1\\); contradiction follows identically.\n\n* **(iii) \\(c=2\\).**  \n  \\(L_{0}\\) contains \\((2,1),(2,2)\\).  \n  Then  \n\n  \\[\n  T=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\}.\n  \\]\n\n  Any two points of \\(T\\) have slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\):\n  vertical pairs \\((1,1)-(1,2)\\), \\((1,1)-(1,3)\\), \\((1,2)-(1,3)\\);\n  horizontal pair \\((3,1)-(1,1)\\) (slope \\(0\\));\n  diagonal pairs \\((1,3)-(3,1)\\) and \\((1,2)-(3,1)\\) have slope \\(-1\\).\n  Hence each sunny line can contain at most one point of \\(T\\), but\n  \\(|T|=4\\); impossible.\n\nThus a vertical non\u2011sunny line is also impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  \\(L_{0}\\) diagonal:  \\(L_{0}:x+y=c\\)\n\nThe values of \\(c\\) for which \\(L_{0}\\) meets \\(S\\) are \\(c=2,3,4\\).\n\n* **(i) \\(c=2\\).**  \n  \\(L_{0}\\) contains only \\((1,1)\\).  \n  Hence  \n\n  \\[\n  T=\\{(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n  \\]\n\n  As in the horizontal case \\(c=2\\), any two points of \\(T\\) have slope\n  \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); the only pair with an allowed slope is\n  \\((1,2)-(2,2)\\) (slope \\(0\\) \u2013 forbidden) and \\((1,2)-(2,3)\\) does not\n  belong to \\(T\\).  Consequently each sunny line can contain at most one\n  point, but \\(|T|=5\\); contradiction.\n\n* **(ii) \\(c=4\\).**  \n  \\(L_{0}\\) contains \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \n  Then  \n\n  \\[\n  T=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}.\n  \\]\n\n  The three pairs give slopes \\(\\infty,0,-1\\); each sunny line can cover at\n  most one point, yet \\(|T|=3\\); contradiction.\n\n* **(iii) \\(c=3\\).**  \n  \\(L_{0}\\) contains \\((1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n  Hence  \n\n  \\[\n  T=\\{(1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n  \\]\n\n  Any two points of \\(T\\) have slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\):\n  vertical \\((1,1)-(1,3)\\), horizontal \\((3,1)-(2,1)\\) (the latter not in\n  \\(T\\) but \\((3,1)-(2,2)\\) gives \\(-1\\)), and the diagonal pairs\n  \\((1,3)-(2,2)\\), \\((2,2)-(3,1)\\) both have slope \\(-1\\).  Thus each\n  sunny line can contain at most one point, while \\(|T|=4\\); contradiction.\n\nTherefore a diagonal non\u2011sunny line also leads to impossibility.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince every possible type of the unique non\u2011sunny line yields a contradiction,\nno arrangement of three distinct lines with exactly two sunny lines can\ncover all points required for \\(n=3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{, the value }k=2\\text{ cannot be attained.}}\n\\]"
