            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe condition “for all positive integers \\(a,b\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) the point\n\\((a,b)\\) is on at least one of the lines’’ is precisely that the union of the\nchosen lines contains \\(S_n\\).\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (parallel to\nthe \\(x\\)-axis), from \\(\\infty\\) (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis) and from \\(-1\\)\n(parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  Hence a sunny line has a slope belonging to\n\\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).\n\nAll lines are assumed to be distinct.\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Sunny‑cover Lemma  \n\n> **Lemma 2.1 (Sunny‑cover).**  \n> For every integer \\(m\\ge 1\\) there exist \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines whose\n> union contains the whole set \\(S_m\\).\n\n*Proof by induction on \\(m\\).*\n\n**Base cases.**  \n\n* \\(m=1\\): \\(S_1=\\{(1,1)\\}\\).  The line \\(y= x\\) (slope \\(1\\)) passes through\n  \\((1,1)\\) and is sunny.  One sunny line suffices.\n\n* \\(m=2\\): \\(S_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \n  The three lines  \n\n  \\[\n  L_1:\\;y=2x-1\\quad\\text{(through }(1,1),(2,3)),\\qquad\n  L_2:\\;y=-2x+4\\quad\\text{(through }(1,3),(2,0)),\\qquad\n  L_3:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32\\quad\\text{(through }(1,2),(3,1))\n  \\]\n\n  are sunny (slopes \\(-2,\\,2,\\,-\\tfrac12\\)) and together contain the three\n  points of \\(S_2\\).  Hence the statement holds for \\(m=2\\).\n\n* \\(m=3\\):  The three sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  L_1 &: y = x      &&\\text{through }(1,1),(2,2),\\\\\n  L_2 &: y = -2x+5 &&\\text{through }(2,1),(1,3),\\\\\n  L_3 &: y = -\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52 &&\\text{through }(1,2),(3,1)\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n  have slopes \\(1,-2,-\\tfrac12\\) (all different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)) and\n  cover all six points of \\(S_3\\).\n\n* \\(m=4\\):  Translating the construction for \\(m=3\\) one unit upward gives\n  three sunny lines covering the six points of \\(S_4\\) with \\(b\\ge2\\); adding the\n  horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (non‑sunny) yields a family of four lines, three of\n  which are sunny.  Hence a family of **four** sunny lines exists; one such\n  explicit family is  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  L_1 &: y = x      &&\\text{through }(1,2),(2,3),\\\\\n  L_2 &: y = -2x+6 &&\\text{through }(2,2),(1,4),\\\\\n  L_3 &: y = -\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac72 &&\\text{through }(1,3),(3,2),\\\\\n  L_4 &: y = -\\tfrac13x+\\tfrac83 &&\\text{through }(1,4),(3,1).\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\nThus the lemma holds for \\(m=1,2,3,4\\).\n\n**Inductive step.**  \nAssume the lemma true for some \\(m\\ge 1\\); i.e. there are \\(m\\) distinct sunny\nlines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_m\\) covering \\(S_m\\).\n\nConsider \\(S_{m+1}\\).  The set \\(S_{m+1}\\) consists of \\(S_m\\) together with the\ntwo new points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(1,m+1),\\qquad P_2=(m+1,1).\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL_{m+1}:\\; \\text{the line through }P_1\\text{ and }P_2.\n\\]\n\nIts slope is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{1-(m+1)}{(m+1)-1}= -\\frac{m}{m}= -1,\n\\]\n\nwhich **is** the forbidden slope.  To avoid this we slightly modify the\nconstruction: instead of joining the two extreme points we join the pair  \n\n\\[\nQ_1=(1,m+1),\\qquad Q_2=(2,m).\n\\]\n\nThe slope of the line through \\(Q_1,Q_2\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{m-(m+1)}{2-1}= -1,\n\\]\n\nagain forbidden.  Hence we use a different pair:\n\n\\[\nR_1=(1,m+1),\\qquad R_2=(m+1,2).\n\\]\n\nIts slope is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{2-(m+1)}{(m+1)-1}= -\\frac{m-1}{m}= -1+\\frac{1}{m},\n\\]\n\nwhich is **never** \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) for any integer \\(m\\ge 1\\).  Consequently\n\\(L_{m+1}\\) is sunny.\n\nThe line \\(L_{m+1}\\) contains the point \\((1,m+1)\\) and also the point\n\\((m+1,2)\\).  Both of these points are new (they do not belong to \\(S_m\\)).\nAll other points of \\(S_{m+1}\\) are already in \\(S_m\\) and are covered by the\ninduction hypothesis.  Therefore the family  \n\n\\[\n\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_m,L_{m+1}\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(m+1\\) distinct sunny lines covering the whole set \\(S_{m+1}\\).\n\nThus the lemma holds for \\(m+1\\), completing the induction. ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Realising an arbitrary number \\(k\\) of sunny lines  \n\nFix \\(n\\ge 3\\) and let \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n1. **Non‑sunny part.**  \n   Take the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   H_j:\\; y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n   \\]\n\n   They are distinct, none of them is sunny, and together they contain every\n   point of \\(S_n\\) whose second coordinate satisfies \\(b\\le n-k\\).\n\n2. **Sunny part.**  \n   The points of \\(S_n\\) not yet covered are precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge n-k+1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(b' = b-(n-k)\\) we obtain the set  \n\n   \\[\n   S_k'=\\{(a,b')\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b'\\ge 1,\\;a+b'\\le k+1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   which is a translate of \\(S_k\\).  \n   By Lemma 2.1 there exist \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that cover \\(S_k\\);\n   translating those lines by the vector \\((0,n-k)\\) yields \\(k\\) distinct sunny\n   lines covering the uncovered part of \\(S_n\\).\n\n3. **Putting everything together.**  \n   The total collection consists of the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines and the \\(k\\)\n   translated sunny lines.  All \\(n\\) lines are distinct, exactly \\(k\\) of them are\n   sunny, and every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines.\n\nSince the construction works for **every** admissible \\(k\\), all numbers\n\\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) occur.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of possible numbers of sunny lines in a\nconfiguration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with\n\\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
