ine, because the condition \\(a+b\\le n+2\\) together with \\(a+b\\ge n+2\\) forces equality.  \nThe line \\(\\ell_{n+1}\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); therefore it is **non‑sunny**.  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}:=\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\cup\\{\\ell_{n+1}\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}\\) consists of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines (the added line has a slope \\(-1\\) and cannot coincide with any line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\), whose slopes are either \\(-1\\) or something else, but the intercepts differ).  \nExactly the same lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) are sunny, so \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}\\) has precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nEvery point of \\(S_{n+1}\\) belongs either to \\(S_n\\) (covered by \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\)) or to the new diagonal (covered by \\(\\ell_{n+1}\\)).  \nThus \\(\\mathcal C(n+1,k)\\) holds for all \\(k\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.2  From \\(\\mathcal L_{n,n}\\) to a family for \\((n+1,n+1)\\)\n\nNow we need a configuration with **all** lines sunny.  \nTake the family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,n}\\) given by the induction hypothesis; by definition every one of its \\(n\\) lines is sunny.\n\nThe new points of \\(S_{n+1}\\) are the points of the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_{n+1}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb N^2\\mid a+b=n+2\\}.\n\\]\n\nChoose the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell^{\\ast}: \\text{ through }(1,n+1)\\text{ and }(n+1,1).\n\\]\n\nIts slope is  \n\n\\[\nm^{\\ast}= \\frac{1-(n+1)}{(n+1)-1}= -1,\n\\]\n\nbut the line joining \\((1,n+1)\\) and \\((n+1,1)\\) is **exactly** the diagonal \\(x+y=n+2\\), which is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore **non‑sunny**.  \nHence we cannot use this line directly.\n\nInstead we take a *slightly tilted* line that still passes through the two extreme points but is not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).  \nPick a real number \\(\\varepsilon\\neq0\\) with \\(|\\varepsilon|<1\\) and define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell^{\\ast}_{\\varepsilon}:\\; y = -x + (n+2) + \\varepsilon (x-1).\n\\]\n\nWhen \\(x=1\\) we obtain \\(y=n+1\\); when \\(x=n+1\\) we obtain \\(y=1+\\varepsilon (n)\\neq1\\).  \nThus \\(\\ell^{\\ast}_{\\varepsilon}\\) passes through \\((1,n+1)\\) and through a point \\((n+1,1+\\varepsilon n)\\) that is **not** a lattice point of \\(S_{n+1}\\).  \nIts slope is \\(-1+\\varepsilon\\neq -1,0,\\infty\\); consequently \\(\\ell^{\\ast}_{\\varepsilon}\\) is sunny.\n\nNow replace in \\(\\mathcal L_{n,n}\\) the (unique) line that contains the point \\((1,n+1)\\) – such a line exists because \\((1,n+1)\\) is not yet covered – by the sunny line \\(\\ell^{\\ast}_{\\varepsilon}\\).  \nAll other points of \\(S_n\\) remain covered, and the whole diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\) is now covered because every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=n+2\\) lies on the line \\(x+y=n+2\\); the only points of this diagonal not yet covered are those with \\(a\\ge2\\).  \nEach of those points belongs to the original family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,n}\\) (recall that \\(\\mathcal L_{n,n}\\) covered all points of \\(S_n\\), and the diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\) is exactly the set of points that were **missing** from \\(S_n\\); however the line \\(\\ell^{\\ast}_{\\varepsilon}\\) already contains the point \\((1,n+1)\\) and, because its slope differs from \\(-1\\), it meets the diagonal \\(x+y=n+2\\) in exactly one point, namely \\((1,n+1)\\).  \nThe remaining points of the diagonal are covered as follows: for each \\(a\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) the point \\((a,n+2-a)\\) lies on the line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,n}\\) that originally passed through \\((a,n+1-a)\\) (which belongs to \\(S_n\\)).  \nSince the two points have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate difference of \\(1\\) and the same \\(y\\)-coordinate difference of \\(-1\\), the line through \\((a,n+1-a)\\) also contains \\((a,n+2-a)\\).  \nHence every point of \\(D_{n+1}\\) is covered.\n\nThe resulting family contains \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, all of which are sunny (the added line is sunny, and the original \\(n\\) lines were sunny).  \nThus \\(\\mathcal C(n+1,n+1)\\) holds.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.3  Completion of the induction\n\nFrom Sections 3.1 and 3.2 we have shown that, assuming the claim for \\(n\\), it holds for \\(n+1\\) for **all** values \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\).  \nSince the claim is true for the base case \\(n=3\\), by induction it is true for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 4.  Determination of the admissible set \\(K_n\\)\n\nWe have proved that for every \\(n\\ge3\\) and every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a configuration satisfying the two conditions of the problem.  \nConversely, a configuration consists of exactly \\(n\\) lines, each of which is either sunny or non‑sunny; therefore the number of sunny lines can never exceed \\(n\\).  \n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nK_n=\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**The final answer is**  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\] \n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\). ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
