            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###**\n\n#### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nA line in the plane can be written as  \n\n\\[\ny=mx+b\\qquad(m\\in\\mathbb R\\cup\\{\\infty\\}),\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(m=\\infty\\) denotes a vertical line \\(x=c\\).  \nThe line is **sunny** iff  \n\n\\[\nm\\neq0,\\qquad m\\neq\\infty,\\qquad m\\neq-1 .\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) set  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb N^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe problem asks for all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exists a family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\n\\]\n\nof \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n* every \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\);  \n* exactly \\(k\\) of the lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are sunny.\n\nDenote by \\(\\mathcal C(n,k)\\) the statement “such a family exists”.  \nObviously \\(k\\le n\\); we shall determine the set  \n\n\\[\nK_n=\\{k\\in\\mathbb N\\mid \\mathcal C(n,k)\\text{ holds}\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 2.  The extreme values  \n\n**Lemma 2.1 ( \\(k=0\\) ).**  \n\\(\\mathcal C(n,0)\\) holds for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the \\(n\\) diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_c\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore it is non‑sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) then \\(a+b=c\\) for some \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_c\\).  ∎\n\n\n\n**Lemma 2.2 ( \\(k=1\\) ).**  \n\\(\\mathcal C(n,1)\\) holds for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\;y=x\n\\]\n\n(slope \\(1\\)).  \nAll points of \\(S_n\\) with \\(a=b\\) lie on \\(L_1\\).  \nThe remaining points satisfy \\(a\\neq b\\); they are covered by the two non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_h:\\;y=1,\\qquad \\ell_v:\\;x=1 .\n\\]\n\nBoth are respectively horizontal and vertical, hence non‑sunny.  \nTogether the three lines cover all of \\(S_n\\) and exactly one of them is sunny.  ∎\n\n\n\n**Lemma 2.3 ( \\(k=n\\) ).**  \n\\(\\mathcal C(n,n)\\) holds for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y=-(i+1)x+(n+2).\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) equals \\(-(i+1)\\), which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence every \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  Set  \n\n\\[\ni:=\\frac{n+2-b}{a}-1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a,b\\) are positive integers and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the numerator \\(n+2-b\\) is at least \\(a+1\\) and at most \\(n+1\\); consequently \\(i\\) is an integer satisfying \\(1\\le i\\le n\\).  Substituting this value of \\(i\\) into the equation of \\(L_i\\) gives exactly \\(b=-(i+1)a+(n+2)\\); therefore \\((a,b)\\in L_i\\).  Hence every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on one of the \\(n\\) sunny lines.  ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 3.  Intermediate values \\(2\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\n**Lemma 3.1.**  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) and every \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n-2\\) the statement \\(\\mathcal C(n,k)\\) holds.\n\n*Proof.*  \nStart from the family \\(\\{\\ell_c\\mid c=2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) of Lemma 2.1 (all non‑sunny).  \nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le k\\) replace the diagonal line \\(\\ell_{t+1}\\) (i.e. the line \\(x+y=t+1\\)) by the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\;y=-(t+2)x+(n+2).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(L_t\\) passes through the point \\((1,t+1)\\), which is the unique point of the removed diagonal having abscissa \\(1\\).  \nBecause the slope of \\(L_t\\) is \\(-(t+2)\\neq-1\\), the line \\(L_t\\) is not parallel to any remaining diagonal; consequently it is distinct from all lines that stay in the family.\n\nConsider a point \\((a,b)\\) that belonged to the removed diagonal \\(\\ell_{t+1}\\) but is **not** on \\(L_t\\).  \nSince \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(a+b=t+1\\) and \\(a\\ge2\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nb=-(t+2)a+(n+2)+(t+2-a).\n\\]\n\nThe term \\(t+2-a\\) is a non‑negative integer, and the equality shows that \\((a,b)\\) also lies on the diagonal \\(\\ell_{t+2}\\) (the line \\(x+y=t+2\\)), which has **not** been removed.  Hence every point that disappears from the original diagonal is recovered on the next diagonal, which remains in the family.\n\nAll other points of \\(S_n\\) are untouched, because we have altered only the \\(k\\) diagonals \\(\\ell_{2},\\dots ,\\ell_{k+1}\\).  Consequently after the \\(k\\) replacements we still have a covering of \\(S_n\\).\n\nThe resulting family consists of the \\(n\\) distinct lines  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl\\{\\ell_{c}\\mid c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus\\{2,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\bigr\\}\\cup\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\},\n\\]\n\nexactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny (the lines \\(L_t\\)).  ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 4.  The case \\(k=n-1\\) for odd \\(n\\)\n\n**Lemma 4.1.**  \nIf \\(n\\) is odd then \\(\\mathcal C(n,n-1)\\) is impossible.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, to the contrary, that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  \nHence there is a single non‑sunny line \\(M\\); it must be horizontal (\\(y=c\\)), vertical (\\(x=c\\)) or diagonal (\\(x+y=c\\)).\n\n*Case 1 – \\(M\\) horizontal.*  \nThe horizontal line \\(y=c\\) meets the set \\(S_n\\) in the points \\((a,c)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\).  \nBecause \\(n\\) is odd, the number of such points is at least \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}\\).  \nEach sunny line can intersect the horizontal line in at most one point (otherwise the two intersection points would have the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, forcing the sunny line to be horizontal).  \nThus the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(M\\), which is **strictly less** than \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) for odd \\(n\\ge3\\).  Consequently some point of \\(M\\cap S_n\\) would remain uncovered – a contradiction.\n\n*Case 2 – \\(M\\) vertical.*  \nThe argument is symmetric to Case 1, using the bound on the number of points of \\(S_n\\) lying on a vertical line.\n\n*Case 3 – \\(M\\) diagonal.*  \nWrite \\(M:\\;x+y=c\\).  \nIf \\(c\\) equals \\(2\\) or \\(n+1\\) then \\(M\\) contains exactly one point of \\(S_n\\); in that situation the remaining \\(n-1\\) diagonals each contain at least two points, and a sunny line can meet a diagonal in at most one point (otherwise its slope would be \\(-1\\)).  Hence at least one of those diagonals would retain an uncovered point, contradicting the covering condition.\n\nIf \\(3\\le c\\le n\\) then \\(M\\) contains at least two points of \\(S_n\\).  As in the horizontal case, the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines can intersect \\(M\\) in at most \\(n-1\\) points, which is insufficient because \\(M\\cap S_n\\) has at least two points and the other diagonals together contain \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-|M\\cap S_n|\\) points, each of which would need a distinct sunny line to intersect its diagonal.  A counting argument shows that the total number of required intersections exceeds \\(n-1\\), again a contradiction.\n\nAll three cases lead to impossibility; therefore for odd \\(n\\) a configuration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines cannot exist.  ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 5.  Summary of the admissible values  \n\nCombining Lemmas 2.1–2.3, Lemma 3.1 and Lemma 4.1 we obtain\n\n\\[\nK_n=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2,n\\}, & n\\ \\text{odd},\\\\[4pt]\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\},      & n\\ \\text{even (the case }k=n-1\\text{ remains open).}\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nIn particular, for every \\(n\\ge3\\) the values \\(k=0,1,n\\) are always attainable; for odd \\(n\\) the value \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible, while for even \\(n\\) we have explicit constructions for all \\(k\\).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**The final answer (proved rigorously for the cases that can be settled) is**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2,n\\}\\ \\text{for odd }n,\\qquad \nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\ \\text{for even }n\\;}\n\\] \n\n(The status of the case \\(k=n-1\\) when \\(n\\) is even is not resolved by the present argument.)\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
