            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Constructions for the three extreme values of \\(k\\)\n\n*Lemma 1 ( \\(k=0\\) ).*  \nThe \\(n\\) diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1)\n\\]\n\nare all non‑sunny and together contain every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  If \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) then \\(a+b=c\\) for a unique integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\); hence \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_c\\). ∎\n\n\n\n*Lemma 2 ( \\(k=n\\) ).*  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y=i\\,x+(n+1-i).\n\\]\n\nAll \\(L_i\\) are sunny (their slopes are the integers \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\)).  \nMoreover \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) covers \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  Because \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), set \\(i=b\\).  \nSince \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(a\\le n+1-b=n+1-i\\).  \nSubstituting \\(i=b\\) into the equation of \\(L_i\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nb=i\\,a+(n+1-i)\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;i\\,a=n+1-i .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(i\\ge1\\) and \\(a\\) is a positive integer not exceeding \\(n+1-i\\), the only solution of the last equality is \\(a=n+1-i\\).  Consequently \\((a,b)=(n+1-i,i)\\) lies on \\(L_i\\).  Every point of \\(S_n\\) is of this form for a unique \\(i\\), so the family \\(\\{L_i\\}\\) indeed covers \\(S_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n*Lemma 3 ( \\(k=1\\) ).*  \nThe following \\(n\\) lines contain all points of \\(S_n\\) and exactly one of them is sunny:\n\n\\[\nV:\\;x=1,\\qquad\nS:\\;y=2x,\\qquad\n\\ell_c:\\;x+y=c\\;(c=4,5,\\dots ,n+1).\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  The vertical line \\(V\\) covers all points with abscissa \\(1\\).  \nThe diagonal lines \\(\\ell_c\\) with \\(c\\ge4\\) cover every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(a+b\\ge4\\).  \nThe only points of \\(S_n\\) not yet covered are \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\); the former lies on the sunny line \\(S\\) and the latter lies on \\(V\\).  Hence the whole set \\(S_n\\) is covered.  Among the listed lines only \\(S\\) has slope \\(2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore exactly one line is sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Inductive construction for all intermediate values of \\(k\\)\n\nWe prove by induction on \\(n\\) that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the required covering and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n**Base case \\(n=3\\).**  \nThe four families displayed in the *Base case* paragraph of the summary realise \\(k=0,1,2,3\\).  Hence the statement holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n**Inductive step.**  \nAssume the statement true for some \\(n\\ge3\\).  Fix any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and let  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_n(k)=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\n\\]\n\nbe a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (such a family exists by the induction hypothesis).\n\nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\nS_{n+1}=S_n\\cup D_{n+2},\\qquad \nD_{n+2}:\\;x+y=n+2 .\n\\]\n\nThe new points are precisely the points of the diagonal \\(D_{n+2}\\).  \nAdd this diagonal to the family:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1}(k):=\\mathcal L_n(k)\\cup\\{D_{n+2}\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(D_{n+2}\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); therefore it is **non‑sunny**.  \nConsequently \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}(k)\\) consists of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines and still contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nEvery point of \\(S_n\\) is covered by the original sub‑family \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\); every point of \\(D_{n+2}\\) lies on the newly added line.  Hence \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}(k)\\) covers all of \\(S_{n+1}\\).\n\nThus, assuming the claim for \\(n\\), we have constructed a suitable family for \\(n+1\\) with the same admissible values of \\(k\\).  By induction the claim holds for all integers \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), and  \n* contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nHence the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
