ingle non‑sunny line, but a non‑sunny line can be either vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\).  \n   - A vertical line can cover at most one of the remaining points because the two points have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates.  \n   - A horizontal line can cover at most one because the two points have distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates.  \n   - A line of slope \\(-1\\) would have to contain both points, which would force their sums \\(a+b\\) to be equal; the two uncovered points have sums \\(3\\) and \\(5\\), a contradiction.  \n   Hence no configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.\n\n   Therefore for \\(n=3\\) the admissible values are precisely \\(\\boxed{\\{0,1,3\\}}\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion of the partial result.**  \n   * For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\) are attainable.  \n   * For \\(n=3\\) the complete set of admissible \\(k\\) is \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).  \n   * The existence (or non‑existence) of configurations for the intermediate values \\(1<k<n\\) when \\(n\\ge 4\\) remains open in the present write‑up.  \n\n   The above statements are proved rigorously; any claim beyond them would require additional constructions that have not been supplied here.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
