ired in step 1.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the proof for the original problem\n\nFix \\(n\\ge3\\) and let \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nPut \\(m:=n-k\\).  \n\n* Use the \\(m\\) vertical lines (2); they are non‑sunny.  \n* Apply the construction of Section 2 with \\(t=n-m=k\\) to the translated set \\(S_{k}\\); this yields exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines covering the remaining points.\n\nAll together we have \\(m+(k)=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every point of \\(S_{n}\\) lies on at least one of them.  \n\nSince the choice of \\(k\\) was arbitrary, **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Final Answer**\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
