they are\n     covered by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   The total number of lines is \\(m+k=n\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n5. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).**  \n   Assume a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.\n   As in step 2 we may replace every non‑sunny line by a vertical line without\n   increasing the number of sunny lines; after this replacement we have\n   \\(m=n-2\\) vertical lines and the uncovered set is a translate of \\(S_2\\).\n\n   In the first (left‑most) column of this translate the points are\n   \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\).  \n   A sunny line is not vertical, hence it meets this column in **at most one**\n   point.  With only two sunny lines we can cover at most two points of the\n   column, but the column contains **three** points \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\) when\n   \\(n\\ge3\\).  Consequently at least one of these three points would remain\n   uncovered, contradicting the hypothesis.  Hence no configuration with\n   exactly two sunny lines exists for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n   (The same argument works for any larger \\(k\\): after removing the\n   \\(n-k\\) vertical lines the first column of the remaining triangle contains\n   \\(k\\) points, and a sunny line can meet that column in at most one point;\n   therefore at least \\(k\\) sunny lines are necessary.  This shows that the\n   minimal possible number of sunny lines is \\(0,1\\) or at least \\(3\\).)\n\nThus the only attainable values of \\(k\\) are \\(0,1\\) and every integer from\n\\(3\\) up to \\(n\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]"
