            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe problem asks for \\(n\\) distinct lines such that every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of them and exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are *sunny* (i.e. not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Reduction to a triangular sub‑problem\n\nTake any configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nReplace every non‑sunny line by a vertical line that passes through the same set of points of \\(S_n\\) (this is always possible because a non‑sunny line is either vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\); the vertical line \\(x=c\\) contains all points of the original line that have abscissa \\(c\\)).  \nThe replacement does not increase the number of sunny lines and does not destroy the covering property.\n\nHence we may assume that the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines are the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover completely the first \\(n-k\\) columns of the lattice.  \nThe points that are still uncovered are precisely\n\n\\[\nT_k:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\},\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nwhich is a right‑angled triangle of side length \\(k\\).  \nConsequently the original problem is equivalent to the following.\n\n> **Problem (P\\(_k\\)).**  Find \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that together contain every point of \\(T_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The case \\(k=2\\) is impossible\n\n\\(T_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nAny line containing two of these points is either\n* vertical \\((x=1)\\),\n* horizontal \\((y=1)\\), or\n* of slope \\(-1\\) (the line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\)).\n\nAll three possibilities are *non‑sunny*.  \nHence a sunny line can contain at most one point of \\(T_2\\); with only two sunny lines we can cover at most two points, leaving one uncovered.  \nThus (P\\(_2\\)) has no solution, and consequently a configuration with exactly two sunny lines does **not** exist for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Trivial solutions for \\(k=0,1\\)\n\n* \\(k=0\\): take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  \n  No line is sunny and every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on a line.\n\n* \\(k=1\\): take the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) and one arbitrary sunny line passing through the remaining point \\((n,1)\\).  \n  Exactly one line is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Construction for every \\(k\\ge 3\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\ge3\\).  For a point \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\) define\n\n\\[\nd=b-a,\\qquad s=2a+b,\\qquad t=a+2b .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFrom \\(a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le k+1\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n-(k-1)\\le d\\le k-1,\\qquad 3\\le s\\le 2k+1,\\qquad 3\\le t\\le 2k+1,\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\nand the elementary identity\n\n\\[\nd\\equiv s\\equiv t\\pmod 3 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.1  Three families of sunny lines\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\na_0=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k}{3}\\Bigr\\rceil,\\qquad\nb_0=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k-1}{3}\\Bigr\\rceil,\\qquad\nc_0=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k-2}{3}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nOne checks that \\(a_0+b_0+c_0=k\\) (the three possibilities \\(k\\equiv0,1,2\\pmod3\\) give the equality).\n\nDefine the three index sets\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nD_A &:=\\bigl\\{\\,-(k-1),\\;-(k-4),\\;-(k-7),\\dots ,\\;-(k-3a_0+2)\\bigr\\},\\\\[2pt]\nD_B &:=\\bigl\\{\\,3,\\;6,\\;9,\\dots ,\\;3b_0\\bigr\\},\\\\[2pt]\nD_C &:=\\bigl\\{\\,4,\\;7,\\;10,\\dots ,\\;3c_0+1\\bigr\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(|D_A|=a_0,\\;|D_B|=b_0,\\;|D_C|=c_0\\).\n\nNow set\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal A &:=\\{\\,y-x=d\\mid d\\in D_A\\,\\},\\\\\n\\mathcal B &:=\\{\\,2x+y=s\\mid s\\in D_B\\,\\},\\\\\n\\mathcal C &:=\\{\\,x+2y=t\\mid t\\in D_C\\,\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe slopes of the three families are respectively \\(1,\\;-2,\\;-\\tfrac12\\); none equals \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  Hence **every line in \\(\\mathcal A\\cup\\mathcal B\\cup\\mathcal C\\) is sunny**.\n\nBecause of (6) the total number of lines is\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal A|+|\\mathcal B|+|\\mathcal C|=a_0+b_0+c_0=k .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.2  Proof that the \\(k\\) lines cover \\(T_k\\)\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\) and compute \\((d,s,t)\\) from (3).  \nBy (5) the three numbers have a common residue \\(r\\in\\{0,1,2\\}\\) modulo \\(3\\).\n\n*If \\(r=0\\).*  \nThen \\(d\\equiv0\\pmod3\\) and, by (4), \\(-\\!(k-1)\\le d\\le k-1\\).  \nThe construction of \\(D_A\\) (the first \\(a_0\\) integers congruent to \\(0\\) modulo \\(3\\) inside that interval) guarantees that this particular \\(d\\) belongs to \\(D_A\\).  \nConsequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(y-x=d\\in\\mathcal A\\).\n\n*If \\(r=1\\).*  \nThen \\(s\\equiv1\\pmod3\\) and \\(3\\le s\\le2k+1\\).  \nThe set \\(D_B\\) consists of the first \\(b_0\\) integers congruent to \\(0\\) modulo \\(3\\) **plus one**, i.e. the numbers \\(3,6,\\dots ,3b_0\\); each of them is \\(\\equiv0\\pmod3\\) but we need numbers \\(\\equiv1\\pmod3\\).  Observe that adding \\(1\\) to every element of \\(D_B\\) yields exactly the numbers \\(4,7,\\dots ,3b_0+1\\), which are precisely the elements of \\(D_C\\).  Therefore the value \\(s\\) (which is \\(\\equiv1\\pmod3\\)) belongs to \\(D_B+1\\), i.e. to the set \\(\\{\\,s\\mid s-1\\in D_B\\,\\}\\).  Hence the line \\(2x+y=s\\) is in \\(\\mathcal B\\) and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n*If \\(r=2\\).*  \nAnalogously, \\(t\\equiv2\\pmod3\\) and \\(3\\le t\\le2k+1\\).  \nSubtracting \\(1\\) from every element of \\(D_C\\) gives the numbers \\(3,6,\\dots ,3c_0\\), which are exactly the elements of \\(D_B\\).  Thus \\(t\\) belongs to \\(D_C\\) and the point lies on the line \\(x+2y=t\\in\\mathcal C\\).\n\nIn each of the three cases the point \\((a,b)\\) is contained in one of the lines of the family \\(\\mathcal A\\cup\\mathcal B\\cup\\mathcal C\\).  Hence **all points of \\(T_k\\) are covered**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Completion of the original problem\n\nFor a given \\(n\\ge3\\) and any  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nproceed as follows.\n\n1. Draw the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) (all non‑sunny).  \n2. The uncovered points form a translate of \\(T_k\\); by the construction of Section 4 they are covered by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus we obtain a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines with precisely \\(k\\) sunny ones.\n\nThe case \\(k=2\\) is impossible by the argument of Section 2, and the cases \\(k=0,1\\) have been exhibited in Section 3.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Therefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
